            "type": "text",
            "text": "Method"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "orderedList",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Heat the oil in a large saucepan over medium heat. Add the onion, carrot, and celery. Cook for 10 minutes until soft."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the garlic, rosemary, and thyme. Cook for 1 minute until fragrant."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the tinned tomatoes. Break them up with a spoon. Cook for 5 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Add the stock and cavolo nero. Bring to a boil, then reduce to a simmer for 20 minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Mash half the beans roughly with a fork. Stir all beans into the pot and simmer for 10 more minutes."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          },
          {
            "type": "listItem",
            "content": [
              {
                "type": "paragraph",
                "content": [
                  {
                    "type": "text",
                    "text": "Season to taste. Remove herb sprigs. Ladle into bowls and drizzle with extra virgin olive oil."
                  }
                ]
              }
            ]
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "troubleshooter",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Soup is too thin",
              "cause": "Not enough starch has been released from the beans",
              "fix": "Mash another cup of beans and stir them back in, then simmer for five more minutes"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Cavolo nero is tough and chewy",
              "cause": "It needs longer cooking time than most greens",
              "fix": "Simmer for an extra 10 minutes until the leaves are completely tender"
            }
          },
          {
            "type": "troubleshootItem",
            "attrs": {
              "symptom": "Soup tastes flat and bland",
              "cause": "The soffritto was not cooked long enough to develop sweetness",
              "fix": "Cook the vegetables for a full 10 minutes before adding anything else"
            }
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "heading",
        "attrs": {
          "level": 2
        },
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "Where this dish lives"
          }
        ]
      },
      {
        "type": "paragraph",
        "content": [
          {
            "type": "text",
            "text": "This soup is from Tuscany in central Italy. It was made by poor families to use up stale bread and leftover soup. The name means reboiled, because it was heated again the next day. You find it in homes and simple restaurants all over the region. It is a winter dish, best eaten when the weather is cold."
          }
        ]
      }
    ]
